Ainars

A puzzle.  Are you able to identify any unusual characteristics associated with the object IDs listed:  277, 390, 453, ...?  Are you trying to do the analysis with any data filters?  Do the filters catch those objects?  Appreciate you making the effort to use Debug mode to look at the dataset.

If the answer is no to the above, please send along your Distance project as a zip file so we can investigate further.

On 26/08/2019 16:04, dubults wrote:

> Hallo again,
>
> Once again I ran into the "objects were never detected" error when running MRDS analysis (double observers, „io” type of model, no covariates yet):
>
> Error in process.data(data, meta.data) :
>   following objects were never detected:277,390,453,694,752,790,1039,1228,1275,1278,1710,1739,1772,1850,1857,2136,2143,2158,2210,2217,2232,2239,2261,2416,2488
> Calls: ddf -> ddf.io <http://ddf.io> -> process.data
> Execution halted
>
> I checked the dataset (including the ddf.dat.r file in Temp folder generated by Distance in debug mode) and did not find any error: each of these objects have exactly 2 records correctly ordered (object x observer 1, object x observer 2 and not intermingled with other objects) and having at least one detection.
> What could be wrong this time?
> I am using Distance version 7.3.
>
> best wishes
> Ainars Aunins
>
>
>
> trešdiena, 2014. gada 16. jūlijs 18:12:27 UTC+3, jeff...@noaa.gov rakstīja:
> > Ainars-
> >
> > The code assumes that the observations are ordered (object x observer 1, object x observer 2, object y observer 1, object y observer 2 etc or (object x observer 1, object y observer 1, object x observer 2, object y observer 2 etc) and objects 895 and 894 are intermingled with other objects for the 2 observers which is causing the code to issue an erroneous message. I thought the code in the Distance interface ordered them but apparently not. I'll add a line of R code to order them in the next version of the package but in the meantime, if you fix those lines it should work fine except that you can't use variables like seat and expert in the dsmodel formula. When I reordered the records and ran your model it threw an error that you used a factor with only one level - which was seat because it only had one value for observer==1. The distance sampling detection function is for the pooled observations - seen by at least one observer.  You can use seat and expert in the mrmodel.  Observer type measures for the distance sampling detection function can only be done in pairs (ie a pair of experts vs a pair of non-experts) because the pairs of data are treated as a unit in the distance sampling detection function.
> >
> > --jeff

